Give the cadence a trigger: publish member sites on Thursdays
The factory could publish and nothing called it. Eight workflows, none of them
the cadence — a finished pipeline that looked like a running one.

`.github/workflows/publish-member-sites.yml`, Thursday 07:00 Denver and on
demand from the Actions tab.

## It does not deploy, and that is the point

It builds member sites, writes them into `site/member-sites/`, and **commits**.
Cloudflare's git build sees the commit and publishes, exactly as it does for any
other change under `site/`.

So there is still **one** thing that puts bytes on the internet. That is the
property deleting `deploy-cloudflare.yml` restored, and it would have been easy
to give it away here by having the cadence deploy directly.

## The signal

You said the signal is what you do not have ready. Nothing in this file
anticipates its shape — no `repository_dispatch` guess, no payload assumptions.
When it exists it becomes another entry under `on:` and **the rest of the file
does not change**. The header says so, so the next session does not invent one.

Thursday is a placeholder that keeps a promise in the meantime. A member who
commits on Friday appears the following Thursday, which is the offer: a day we
can keep rather than an immediacy we cannot.

## Two bugs, both found by checking rather than reading

**A new member site is untracked, and `git diff` does not see untracked paths.**
I copied the sync workflows' commit idiom, which works for them because
`cablecast.json` already exists. Measured:

```
git diff --quiet on untracked new site: SEES NOTHING
git add then diff --cached:             DETECTS IT
```

As written it would have reported *"nothing to publish"* on precisely the run
that published somebody's site for the first time — committed nothing, exited 0,
green check. It now stages first and compares `--cached`.

`site/member-sites/.gitkeep` is committed so that pathspec always matches
something, including on a run with no tenants. Jekyll ignores dotfiles, so it is
not published.

**`| tee` was swallowing the exit status.** GitHub's default shell is
`bash -e {0}` without `pipefail`, so the step's status comes from `tee`, which
always succeeds. A build that failed **our** site would have reported green.
`shell: bash` is the only way to get `-eo pipefail`; it is set, with the reason
in a comment.

## What a failing member does

Nothing, to anybody else. `build-sites.py` builds each site independently and
exits zero when only tenants failed, so one member's unparseable data file
cannot make everybody else miss the day. The failures land in the run summary.

## Verified

- **Zero-tenant steady state commits nothing** — ran the builder, staged, and
  confirmed the detection reports no change once `.gitkeep` is committed.
- Workflow YAML parses; `submodules: recursive` on checkout so pinned tenants
  hydrate and unpinned ones report `absent` rather than failing.
- `python3 bin/test_build_sites.py` — 44 tests, OK.
- `check-template.py` round trip passes.
- `docs/member-sites.md` updated to name the trigger and say it does not deploy.

## Note

This is the one item from the debrief I listed as *"looks finished, is inert."*
It is now triggered. The other one — the journal engine mount — is still not
done and is still an instruction rather than a deferral.

